// `.pop()` on an `i64[]`. The checker accepts it — `.pop` is constrained to an array and an
// `i64[]` is one — and what happens next depends on WHERE THE RECEIVER LIVES.
// Should print 2.
//
// THIS SPELLING IS SILENT, which makes the probe a clause-1 row and not only a clause-2 one:
// a local (or `const`) receiver gives `vl check` rc 0 and then a module the engine refuses
// to load — `Invalid input WebAssembly code … type mismatch: expected (ref null $type), found
// (ref $type)`. Three lines, no other list in the program, nothing else needed.
//
// THE PARAMETER SPELLING IS LOUD. Move the pop into `function trim(a: i64[]) { a.pop() }` and
// the same program refuses at emit with `emitProgram: .pop receiver is not a list`. One
// missing ladder, two arms, and only one of them says so.
//
// THE ELEMENT TYPE IS THE VARIABLE. `i32[]` and `string[]` pop and run. `f64[]` refuses with a
// THIRD message (`emitProgram: .pop but list type not collected`), and because `emitFail` does
// not halt, an `f64[]` pop beside a popped `i32[]` also lands as check-clean invalid wasm.
//
// FOUND BY: `std:fmt`'s exact big-integer arithmetic (serde stage 0), which holds its limbs
// in an `i64[]` and therefore cannot trim high zero limbs. It carries a `bnLen` scan over the
// limbs instead, and its header says to delete that the day this probe runs.
const a: i64[] = [1, 2, 3]
a.pop()
print(a.length)
